<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Masudi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 896–956</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as al-Mas'udi, Mas'udi, Abu al-Hasan al-Mas'udi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Masudi was an Arab historian and geographer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, often called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>. He travelled widely through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the Caspian lands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and his Meadows of Gold, completed in the 940s, combines world history with descriptions of peoples and lands from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> to the kingdoms of India. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> around 956.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-masudi.jpg" alt="Al-Masudi" width="320" height="566" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hubertl · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NHM_Bellariastra%C3%9Fe_side_risalit_right_-_Al_Masudi_-_Emmerich_Alexius_Swoboda_3890.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Baku on the Absheron peninsula was known for its oil wells and naphtha, which medieval geographers such as <b>al-Mas'udi</b> described, and for salt, while its harbour linked Shirvan to the trade of the Caspian with Iran, Central Asia and the Volga.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the same century <b>Al-Masudi</b> gave the Arabic translation's name as Alf Khurafa, generally known as Alf Layla, and named the characters Shirāzd and Dināzād. No physical evidence of the Hezār Afsān survives.</blockquote><a href="/work/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Lakz is known chiefly from Arab writers of the 10th century such as <b>al-Masudi</b>, who describe a numerous and warlike people in the mountains west of Derbent, generally identified with the ancestors of the Lezgins.</blockquote><a href="/state/lakz">Lakz</a></li></ul></section>
